<!--
  ============================================================================
  PERCEPTION SERVICE — MANIFEST + STRINGS ADDITIONS
  ============================================================================

  Add the following to your existing files. DO NOT replace the whole files —
  these are snippets to merge in.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  1. app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Inside <application>, add:

      <service
          android:name="com.amar.vault.agent.perception.PerceptionService"
          android:exported="false"
          android:label="@string/perception_service_label"
          android:permission="android.permission.BIND_ACCESSIBILITY_SERVICE">
          <intent-filter>
              <action android:name="android.accessibilityservice.AccessibilityService" />
          </intent-filter>
          <meta-data
              android:name="android.accessibilityservice"
              android:resource="@xml/perception_service_config" />
      </service>

  No top-level <uses-permission> is required for AccessibilityService itself —
  the permission is granted by the user toggling the service on in Settings,
  not via a runtime permission dialog.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  2. app/src/main/res/xml/perception_service_config.xml
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Copy the perception_service_config.xml file I generated into
  app/src/main/res/xml/.

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  3. app/src/main/res/values/strings.xml
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Add these three strings. The description is shown to users in
  Settings → Accessibility when they decide whether to enable the service.
  Keep it HONEST — users ignoring this prompt is a misfeature, not a feature.

      <string name="perception_service_label">Amar Vault Agent</string>

      <string name="perception_service_summary">
          Lets Amar Vault read what\'s on screen so the on-device agent can execute
          tasks you request (like sending a message in WhatsApp).
      </string>

      <string name="perception_service_description">
          Amar Vault uses this service to see the contents of apps you choose to
          automate, and to perform taps and text entry on your behalf.

          All processing stays on your device. Amar Vault never sees screens from
          apps you haven\'t explicitly asked it to automate, and no screen content
          is sent to any server.

          You can disable this service at any time from Settings → Accessibility.
      </string>

  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  4. Application class wiring
  ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

  In your VaultApplication (or equivalent Hilt Application subclass), add to
  onCreate():

      // Agent perception wiring. The AccessibilityService is created by Android,
      // not by Hilt, so we inject shared state via its bind() method once the
      // service connects. If the service is enabled before our Application.onCreate
      // runs (rare but possible on process restart), rebind() handles it.
      val snapshotCache = SnapshotCache()
      val watchlist = PackageWatchlist()
      AgentStateHolder.init(snapshotCache, watchlist)
      PerceptionService.rebind(snapshotCache, watchlist)

  You'll want a small AgentStateHolder (pure Kotlin singleton) or equivalent Hilt
  @Singleton bindings so the rest of the Control Layer can also reach
  SnapshotCache / PackageWatchlist. I can generate this in the next turn along
  with AgentModule (Hilt) — flagging now so the current files don't reference
  undefined injection points.
-->